  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nTuesday-Thursday:\nThe upper-level ridge will gradually begin to retreat westward \ninto Tuesday with an approaching cold front sinking south into \nthe area. Ahead of the front, hot and humid conditions persist. \nHighs are progged to reach into the lower/middle 90s (north) to \nupper 90s/near 100 (south). Additionally, heat index values push\n100 to 110 once again. It is reasonable that an upgrade to an \nExtreme Heat Warning may be warranted across southeast KS into \nsouthern MO if temperatures/dewpoints trend a bit higher. This \nwill be our last day of the widespread heat wave.\n\nAs front approaches, moisture influx increases into the region.\nThis should support scattered shower and thunderstorm chances \n(10-30%) on Tuesday afternoon and evening, though there remains \nsome uncertainty on the extent of coverage. CAMs remain \ndivergent on solutions, with only a handful showing scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms across the area. It is reasonable that\nforcing and moisture should be sufficient for at least some \ncoverage of PoPs, particularly along and north of Interstate \n44. Likewise, this area has lower confidence for reaching Heat \nAdvisory criteria given the potential PoPs and associated cloud \ncover. Wind shifts occur through the afternoon and evening, with\nslightly cooler weather filtering into the area behind the \npassage.\n\nAdditional shower and thunderstorm chances (20-50%) take shape\ninto Tuesday night and Wednesday, and persist through Thursday.\nThis is associated with the area being in the eastern periphery\nof the ridge, with active northwest flow over the region. A\nseries of shortwave translate through the pattern and override\nan existing boundary. Severe weather is not expected with any of\nthe systems. However, localized heavy rainfall remains possible\ngiven PWATs approaching 1.8 to 2.0+ inches. This will be a\nscenario where some locations may remain completely dry, while\nanother location sees a quick 1 to 2 inches of rainfall. Given\nthis localized threat, WPC captures our area in a Marginal (1 \nof 4) Excessive Rainfall Outlook for Tuesday. We are not\nexpecting all day washouts Tuesday through Thursday. Otherwise,\nmore seasonable temperatures return through mid to late week. \nHighs in the upper 80s to lower 90s, with perhaps some middle \n90s sneaking into southeast KS and far southwest MO.\n\nThursday Night-Sunday:\nBy Thursday night, a stronger system moves into the region.\nEnsemble guidance still varies on timing and location of this\nsystem, but the general consensus is for increasing shower and\nthunderstorm chances (40-70%) overnight Thursday into Friday.\nLikewise to mid-week, the environment will be supportive of\nlocalized heavy rainfall that may lead to a few instances of\nflash flooding. Rain chances look to persist through Friday\nnight, before diminishing to the east through Saturday. In\ngeneral, a cooler weekend is in the forecast with highs ranging\nfrom lower to upper 80s",
